// Placing a node against several things at once.
//
// Naming more than one target places the node against the box that just bounds
// them all. Both forms take a list, and they differ in what the region is for.
//
// A direction uses the region as a floor: "right of one and three" clears the
// furthest of them, which is what the solver's longest paths already computes,
// so it works whatever the targets are and wherever they sit. Note that the
// annotation says nothing about its own vertical position — that falls out of
// the region, which is the whole point of naming a region rather than a box.
//
// An alignment uses the region as a position, and "top level with" takes an
// edge of it rather than its center line. The three stacked boxes are not
// inside a container, so the region they bound is not known until the diagram
// has been solved once; it is measured off that first solution and pinned.
//
// The benchmark exercises the container case, where the region is a constant.
// This file is the coverage for the other one.

box one    "first"
box two    "second"                 below one
box three  "third with a longer label"  below two

note spans  "level with all three, and clear of the widest"  right of one and three  wrap: 24  gap: wide
note tops   "top level with the second box"                  right of two  top level with two  wrap: 20  gap: tight

box beside  "centered on the top two"  right of spans  level with one and two  gap: wide
